   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">R has been accepted by Google to be a GSoC mentoring organization again in 2018!<div><br></div><div>Now the work for everyone else in the organization picks up.</div><div><br></div><div>Potential mentors: if you haven&#39;t already done so, please polish your project idea pages on the wiki here:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://github.com/rstats-gsoc/gsoc2018/wiki/table%20of%20proposed%20coding%20projects\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/rstats-gsoc/gsoc2018/wiki/table%2520of%2520proposed%2520coding%2520projects&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792351371817000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2vDgM4Ug2-_vRtMCyNQSFX\">https://github.com/rstats-gsoc/gsoc2018/wiki/table%20of%20proposed%20coding%20projects</a></div><div><br></div><div>Project idea pages are a non-optional part of the process, required both by Google and by R GSoC policies.  These projects are competitive, and are chosen based on all of the material prov ided by students and by mentors.</div><div><br></div><div>Potential students: now is the time to start contacting mentors for posted ideas, providing patches, submitting test results, etc. to really show your interest in working on an R GSoC project in 2018.  You can even start working on your project application.  The best and most successful student applications typically run to about 10 pages if printed, so it is hard to write the entire thing in the space of the application period.  Expect more idea pages to be posted or finished over the next week or two.</div><div><br></div><div>If you have questions, please direct them to the list if they are general, or to Toby or me if they need to be handled privately.</div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div><br></div><div>Brian</div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">---------- Forwarded message ----------<br>From: <b class=\"gmail_sendername\">Google Summer of Code</b><br>Date: Mon, Feb 12, 2018 at 11:10 AM<br>